WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.567
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-nominated

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:04.334
and Golden Globe-winning actor
you know from shows like

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:05.801
"Deadwood" and "American Gods."

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:08.167
He stars in
"John Wick: Chapter 4,"

00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:10.601
which is in theaters
and IMAX March 24th.

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:12.033
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:15.033
-Where will it end?

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:17.334
Huh?

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:19.868
-With him dead.

00:00:19.868 --> 00:00:20.968
-Oh.

00:00:20.968 --> 00:00:23.167
And then?

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:25.000
Hmm?

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.100
Have you learned nothing?

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.567
They will replace him
before the body's cold.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.534
Hercules had his hydra.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:37.000
You have the table,
except you'll run out of bullets

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.968
before they run out of heads.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:41.534
-Please welcome back to
the show our friend Ian McShane!

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:43.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.534 --> 00:00:53.534
♪♪

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:58.601
♪♪

00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:01.200
-Welcome back, Ian.
-Thank you.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.734
B.A. just informed me
my bag has finally arrived.

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:05.701
-Oh, congratulations.
-That's why I'm dressed --

00:01:05.701 --> 00:01:07.467
But it's a lovely day for ducks,
isn't it?

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.167
-It is a lovely day.
-Yeah.

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:10.367
-You know what?
It's so exciting.

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:11.968
First of all,
you look fit as a fiddle.

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:13.200
-Thank you.
-And last time you were here

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.167
for "John Wick,"
you know, you were saying,

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.934
"This is the third one.
I hope they make a fourth one.

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:20.901
And I hope they give me some
action sequences myself."

00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:23.033
-They did, but they --
they cut them out, apparently,

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:26.033
because they were [bleep]
No, really, they were --

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:29.100
I started learning
this ancient form

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.834
of martial arts called McThai.

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:33.634
It's a Scottish form of --
-Okay.

00:01:33.634 --> 00:01:36.968
-Well, no, you wear a kilt,
naked, and you run at them,

00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:38.033
and it terrifies them.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:39.501
-It's called McThai?

00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:41.467
-Well, I ran at Donnie Yen,
and he ran the other way.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.334
-Wow.
And they cut it all out?

00:01:43.334 --> 00:01:44.968
-Because I said --
"Ach, away wit' you now!

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:47.267
I'm comin' for you now!"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.200
-Yeah, that might not quite fit
in "John Wick."

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.267
-No, I don't think it'd fit,
really.

00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:52.267
-Yeah, yeah.
-I saw it last week

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:55.534
at the London premiere,
and it's fantastic, actually.

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:58.234
I don't say that.
I'm pretty tough on myself

00:01:58.234 --> 00:02:00.601
in other movies,
but it really is spectacular.

00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:03.601
Chad and Keanu
have outdone themselves,

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:06.234
I mean, with the story,
and three hours,

00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:09.367
it feels like an hour and a half
of smash, bang, wallop action,

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:13.534
combined with some masterful
nods to great movies of history,

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:14.667
other action movies.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.601
And all the newcomers
like Donnie,

00:02:17.601 --> 00:02:20.234
like Shamier Anderson,
Bill Skarsgard,

00:02:20.234 --> 00:02:22.234
they're all fantastic.
-It's really exciting.

00:02:22.234 --> 00:02:23.467
You know, this is,
well, you know, a movie

00:02:23.467 --> 00:02:24.601
that kind of
came out of nowhere...

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:26.000
-Yeah.
-...when it came out in 2014.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.434
And, you know, one of the things
I love about it is,

00:02:28.434 --> 00:02:30.434
your character keeps being
a bigger part of the story,

00:02:30.434 --> 00:02:32.334
which is really fun,
being a fan of yours.

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:34.534
Are you surprised
at what a phenomenon

00:02:34.534 --> 00:02:35.601
it's become in almost a decade?

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:36.801
-You're always surprised,
I think.

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:38.734
You know, it was a very tight
indie movie

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:40.868
which we made
at the end of 2013.

00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:42.434
It had some other great actors
in it --

00:02:42.434 --> 00:02:45.234
Willem Dafoe, Michael Nyqvist.

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:47.601
And it turned out -- I remember,
I was filming a year later,

00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:50.200
doing a movie down in Atlanta,
and it opened.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.534
And, you know, you --

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:54.300
I went into the early show
down in some cinema there.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.601
You know when you see a movie,

00:02:56.601 --> 00:02:59.400
the first 10 seconds
if you're in good hands or not.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.834
-Uh-huh.
-And you know at the open,

00:03:00.834 --> 00:03:03.234
I thought, "Oh, yeah.
Oh, yeah, we're in good hands."

00:03:03.234 --> 00:03:05.334
And then two years later,
he called,

00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:06.567
"Do you want
to make another one?"

00:03:06.567 --> 00:03:07.801
And then, "Do you want
to make another one?"

00:03:07.801 --> 00:03:10.167
And here we are now,
the fourth one.

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:13.100
And I think Chad
has really outdone --

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.601
I mean it's really --

00:03:14.601 --> 00:03:17.234
I was sitting there, and I took
my kids and grandkids --

00:03:17.234 --> 00:03:19.267
my grandkids, who are
of the age now

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:21.400
they can watch things like
"Deadwood."

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.601
[Bleep] -- you couldn't even
say those words on television.

00:03:23.601 --> 00:03:25.300
I mean you say them now
on this show.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.167
-I -- we do say them.
-It's appalling.

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:29.567
I think it's appalling behavior.
-My -- I think I let --

00:03:29.567 --> 00:03:30.767
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:31.868
-What will the children --

00:03:31.868 --> 00:03:33.434
-Well, I learned it
from watching you.

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:34.701
-I know you did.
That's what I mean.

00:03:34.701 --> 00:03:36.400
-Okay?
But I'll tell you this.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.634
I'll let my kids watch
"Deadwood" when they're like 28.

00:03:38.634 --> 00:03:40.467
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.467 --> 00:03:42.567
But your grandkids, I mean,
they must love --

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:44.267
-They loved it.
Oh, they had an absolute ball.

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:45.934
Yeah, and they got
to meet Keanu afterwards.

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:47.300
-Uh-huh.
-I think the youngest one,

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.868
Raphael, pitched him
a number five.

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:51.601
-Uh-huh.
-I said, "Well, give Keanu

00:03:51.601 --> 00:03:53.667
a chance to recover.
I mean, he's already --

00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:56.968
He's only killed 5,394 people
in this movie.

00:03:56.968 --> 00:03:58.200
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.634
Big population to go through.

00:03:59.634 --> 00:04:02.534
-Now, you --
This is obviously --

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:05.734
You've had this long,
fantastic, colorful career.

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:08.367
But I didn't realize you were
here in New York City,

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:11.200
doing some theater work
late '60s.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.467
-The summer of love.
-You were here summer of love.

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:14.634
-Summer of love.
-How was it?

00:04:14.634 --> 00:04:15.901
Was New York City everything
they said it was?

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:17.400
-Well, we came
a little after the sum--

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.934
The autumn of love, we were.
-Okay.

00:04:18.934 --> 00:04:21.000
[ Laughter ]
-We came in October.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.734
But I remember,
yeah, oh, it was fantastic.

00:04:22.734 --> 00:04:23.968
It was a play called
"The Promise,"

00:04:23.968 --> 00:04:25.100
and we'd done it in London,

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.734
Ian McKellen and I.
-Wow.

00:04:26.734 --> 00:04:28.634
-And Judi Dench
did the original.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.968
And then we came to New York
'cause Judy was doing "Cabaret,"

00:04:30.968 --> 00:04:34.167
the musical in London,
so Eileen Atkins.

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:36.501
And I think America
still harbored the suspicions

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:39.734
about Russians, because we had
a great rehearsal period, open.

00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:42.033
And, you know, it's weird
when you've had a smash hit

00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:44.000
in London, and then audiences,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.367
I think with 36 people
in the first matinee.

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:49.133
And then most of them
were Puerto Rican students

00:04:49.133 --> 00:04:50.534
learning English.
-Oh, wow.

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:51.701
[ Laughter ]
No, I'm serious.

00:04:51.701 --> 00:04:53.767
They were learning --
'cause you can hear them

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:56.567
talking on with you, going --
I go, "Can you hear something?"

00:04:56.567 --> 00:04:57.767
McKellen, he goes,
"Can you hear something?"

00:04:57.767 --> 00:04:59.534
I said, "Yeah, they're learning
English with us.

00:04:59.534 --> 00:05:01.767
'Cause they think
we speak English

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:04.200
better than Americans do."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.801
But it was --
But I remember seeing "Hair"

00:05:05.801 --> 00:05:07.267
at the Public Theater.
-Uh-huh.

00:05:07.267 --> 00:05:08.734
-First out.
I used to go down to

00:05:08.734 --> 00:05:10.934
the Electric Circus
and down in the Village.

00:05:10.934 --> 00:05:12.200
Half price with no shoes.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.634
-If you didn't have shoes,
it was half price?

00:05:13.634 --> 00:05:15.033
-That's it.
If you didn't have shoes.

00:05:15.033 --> 00:05:16.534
-Well that's -- that's -- that's
the autumn of love for you.

00:05:16.534 --> 00:05:18.534
-I did have shoes.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:18.534 --> 00:05:21.033
-Now, I've always appreciated
this about you,

00:05:21.033 --> 00:05:22.667
because you're one of my
favorite actors

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:24.634
to watch on-screen.
But I also know that you find it

00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:27.567
very boring when actors
talk about their technique.

00:05:27.567 --> 00:05:30.200
-[ Mock snores ]
[ Laughter ]

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:31.434
Unbelievable.

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:33.000
-And you don't like being
asked about it, either.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.667
-I certainly don't.
-Yeah.

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:37.067
-But if you insist,
I find that my latest part --

00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:38.934
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.934 --> 00:05:42.767
I --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:44.334
-You don't like talking about
motivation

00:05:44.334 --> 00:05:47.067
or any of those things.
-Well, the first thing I said,

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:49.000
it was like, Billy Wilder was
asked, and Billy Wilder said,

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.968
"The check."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:53.400
And, you know, for a guy who's
a genius like that, I thought,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.100
"That's a fair enough answer.
I'll stick with that one."

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.667
-Yeah, that's not bad.
Well, let me tell you,

00:05:57.667 --> 00:05:59.234
they're getting everything
they pay for

00:05:59.234 --> 00:06:01.067
every time they put you
on screen.

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:03.601
Now, I know you have a passion.
We've talked about in the past.

00:06:03.601 --> 00:06:05.968
I've been lucky enough
to know you for a bit now.

00:06:05.968 --> 00:06:07.934
You're a football fan,
to this day.

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:10.667
You're a Man United fan.
And your dad actually played.

00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:13.567
-My dad played for them in the
'50s, yeah, Manchester United.

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:15.434
So I've --
You know, I was --

00:06:15.434 --> 00:06:17.667
I've been watching the club
for like,

00:06:17.667 --> 00:06:19.133
God, 60-odd years, you know?

00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:20.467
-And did you have a moment there
when you were young,

00:06:20.467 --> 00:06:21.634
where you thought, "Maybe I'll
follow in his footsteps"?

00:06:21.634 --> 00:06:23.067
-Oh, no.
I never thought I'd play.

00:06:23.067 --> 00:06:25.300
No, I mean, I think, you know,
my dad wasn't one of those,

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:26.834
like, "You will play football."

00:06:26.834 --> 00:06:28.767
I mean, you can't unless
you're -- yeah, I was fine.

00:06:28.767 --> 00:06:30.434
I was good.
I was a good little player

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:32.934
when I played at junior level,
whatever, till 11.

00:06:32.934 --> 00:06:36.167
Broke my leg and then sort of
lost interest after that.

00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:37.767
I mean, I love the game now,
though.

00:06:37.767 --> 00:06:40.300
I love the game.
I mean, again, acting,

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.734
there wasn't like --
acting didn't take place over --

00:06:43.734 --> 00:06:45.767
over -- over football.
You know what I mean?

00:06:45.767 --> 00:06:47.267
[ Laughter ]
I was in football,

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:49.334
but I broke my leg, and I
decided to become an actor.

00:06:49.334 --> 00:06:51.100
I mean, how [bleep] stupid
is that?

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:53.067
-They were probably --
-I was just in a school play,

00:06:53.067 --> 00:06:54.834
and the guy at school said,

00:06:54.834 --> 00:06:56.534
"You know, you're not bad
at this, you know?"

00:06:56.534 --> 00:06:59.267
And I found myself very
comfortable being on the stage.

00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:00.634
That's all.
And he said,

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:01.834
"I think you should apply
for RADA."

00:07:01.834 --> 00:07:04.367
And my mom and dad
were both terrific people.

00:07:04.367 --> 00:07:06.367
And my dad -- "Absolutely."
So I went off.

00:07:06.367 --> 00:07:09.200
I went at 17
for a RADA audition with my mom,

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.100
and I got in, and that was it.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.400
Then I went when I was --

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.000
Yeah, when I was just 18,
and left when I was 19.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.634
I did a movie.
That was --

00:07:16.634 --> 00:07:18.334
So here we are later,
still doing it.

00:07:18.334 --> 00:07:20.400
-And were you -- were they
the kind of parents,

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:21.968
did they like
seeing you onstage?

00:07:21.968 --> 00:07:23.000
Did they like seeing --
-No. My mother --

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.667
My mother
was always worried about

00:07:24.667 --> 00:07:26.033
what people would say about,
you know --

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:27.834
-Interesting.
-Well, my -- my dad,

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:30.934
he was a footballer.
My mother really didn't like

00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:32.834
the publicity
that surrounded my dad,

00:07:32.834 --> 00:07:34.434
you know, 'cause people
criticize you.

00:07:34.434 --> 00:07:36.100
-Yeah.
-It's their perfect right.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.434
If you choose to be in public,
it's like, you know,

00:07:38.434 --> 00:07:40.934
everybody can say, "Why don't
you quietly go away?"

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:42.667
if they don't like you.
Everyone --

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:44.701
No, they have,
the perfect right, whatever.

00:07:44.701 --> 00:07:46.200
But it's like
she couldn't stand that.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:47.934
Me, it's just water
off a duck's back.

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:49.267
As an actor
or a football player,

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:52.100
it's what you do for a living.
But my dad was lovely.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.467
He just smiled and said,
"Oh, have a good time, son."

00:07:54.467 --> 00:07:56.133
[ Laughter ]
"Have a good time."

00:07:56.133 --> 00:07:57.567
-Well, I hope
you've had a good time.

00:07:57.567 --> 00:07:59.634
It's certainly been a lot of fun
watching it.

00:07:59.634 --> 00:08:01.067
-Not bad.
-And it is just wonderful

00:08:01.067 --> 00:08:02.567
having you here with us.
Thank you so much, Ian.

00:08:02.567 --> 00:08:04.801
-Nice to see you, Seth.
Thank you.

00:08:04.801 --> 00:08:07.901
-"John Wick: Chapter 4,"
theaters and IMAX, March 24th.

00:08:07.901 --> 00:08:09.467
We'll be right back
with Ms. Pat.

